Z||000000||Richard Parnell||09-14-1999||04:29 PM||||I drive  an '87 SC w/4 cyl & 5 spd. The Haynes manual I have only barely mentions the distributerless distributer set up and virtually no other info on the DIS engine setup.  I tried to find the fuel filter to no avail.  The only thing I can see is that the fuel goes to a large coffee can sized device & from there to the engine and back again.  Does anyone know where I could obtain some sort of documentation for this strange engine so I can work on it with a bit more guidance. I feel like  I'm stumbling around in the dark. Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||09-14-1999||04:51 PM||||While there may be a small filter in the 700 TBI unit, the main filter is in a line under the car. Yes, it's fairly large.<P>There is very little to do to a DIS motor. The whole thing is controlled by the ECM. The only moving parts in the control system are the throttle linkage/butterfly, and the TPS.<P>One of the first things you might want to do is replace the TPS. this sensor does have moving parts and is likely very worn. You can save some potential headaches really quickly.<P>There is not allot of doumentation at all on these motors becuase there is not a whole lot of things to be done to them. If a sensor breaks the ECM will pretty much tell you what one. (The AutoXray GM scanner will tell you for sure.)<P>There is a book, from petersons I think, that describes the 700 TBI, and a number of others.<P>There are no after market replacements for the 700 TBI. There are Accell coils, and an MSD ignition controller available to enhance the ignition. The ignition really doesn't need much help if it's in good working order.<P>One note.... Don't put platinum spark plugs in it. The DIS "waste spark" burns out the tiny center electrode.<P>Another Note...... If there is fuel leaking from the "dome" on the trunk side of the TBI, then rebuild the TBI. The diaprham for the fuel pressure regulator is almost as much as the rebuild kit, so it doesn't make sense to only change the diapram which is also in the rebuild kit.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Richard Parnell||09-14-1999||05:01 PM||||Wow! Talk about fast replies. I've been feeling like the lonely guy driving some strange vehicle that was dropped off on the planet by visiting aliens bent on making us crazy. Thanks very much.  These guys are good.||||reg||6
Z||000003||theogre||09-14-1999||08:04 PM||||engine/transmision wise there is nothing odd or off beat about Fiero. These are the same engines/trannies used in millions of GM product. Anyone tells you differant is a liar or a twit. The only thing unique in the drive train is it's layout. All diagnostic and other service practices are the same with One exception.<P>The exception is the cooling system..... You fill the system with some special rules. Don't follow those rules and you got a major mess. Read the haynes book.... the filling procedure is in it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ozzy||09-14-1999||11:12 PM||||Hey Richard...and welcome to the best fiero site going!!! Lots of well informed people here who are willing to help with your problems. I've not owned my '88 4 cyl long and my money is always on "the ogre". Has helped me solve many problems on my lil' 4 banger! (No ogre I don't want money!) Many others have also given me good insight to the many idiosyncrasies associated with this little 'gem'! (mine is broke right now)<P>Ozzy||||reg||12
Z||000005||ChadMan||09-15-1999||09:00 AM||||What's the matter with your Coupe, Ozzy?<P>Personally, I love the DIS motor.  My 88 coupe is much more reliable than my 84 was.  I found good information on the DIS motor in Chilton's book on the Cutlass Ciera at the local library.<P>But I'd like to know where the extra 6 hp GM claims for the DIS motor is.  My 84 coupe was quicker (probably the 4.10 gears).||||reg||1
Z||000006||Richard Parnell||09-15-1999||03:47 PM||||I know I'll probably hear about this from lots of Fiero purists, but I've been making plans for putting a V8 in my coupe.  I've got the engine (Chevy 350 w/ 4 barrel) and have just been gathering as much info before starting as I can from all the very knowledgable sources out here on the net.  I must say thought that this site is the best.  The main reasons for doing the V8 Fiero is I rode in one (WOW), I'm an old hot rodder and want to drive a nice sounding V8 again, also the only thing the computer will be doing is running my dash lights.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||09-15-1999||04:42 PM||||Mine is an 87 SC.... Autotrany.... You must have the stick. The TC torq multiplication effect makes mine real quick for a 4 cyl.||||reg||2
Z||000008||theogre||09-15-1999||11:41 PM||||the book I was thinking about above is also Haynes. One of the techbook series. It's book 10220(2111) and covers both the 300 unit used thru 86 and the 700 TBI unit used from 87 on.||||reg||1
